# Invite Only - TryHackMe: Invite Only
Date: 29 September 2026
Difficulty: Easy
Category: Threat intelligence

# Scenario
As a SOC analyst at the managed server provider TrySecureMe, I supported an L3 analyst during incident response. An L1 analyst had escalated two suspicious indicators, a flagged IP address and a SHA256 hash. My task was to investigate both with the TryDetectThis2.0 threat intelligence platform and turn the findings into usable threat intelligence.

Flagged indicators:
```
IP:     101[.]99[.]76[.]120
SHA256: 5d0509f68a9b7c415a726be75a078180e3f02e59866f193b0a99eee8e39c874f
```

# Tools Used
- TryDetectThis2.0 threat intelligence search application
- Check Point Research public threat report
- Google for report lookup

# What I Did
- Started the lab machine and launched TryDetectThis2.0 from the desktop.
- Searched the flagged SHA256 hash and reviewed the file report for name, type, detections and classification.
- Checked the related information panel for contacted domains, contacted IPs, execution parents, bundled files and dropped files.
- Pivoted on the second execution parent (installer.exe) to list the malicious files it dropped.
- Searched the flagged IP and reviewed vendor detections, geolocation, ASN and the community section.
- Used the community comment to identify the malware family and the original report.
- Read the Check Point Research report to identify the cookie stealing tool, phishing technique and redirect platform.

