# What I Found
- The flagged hash belongs to syshelpers.exe, a Win32 EXE of 214 KB flagged by 51 of 72 vendors and classified as trojan.tedy/fxnp (trojan, downloader).
- The file contacted trusted cloud services such as bitbucket.org and bbuseruploads.s3.amazonaws.com, which fits the campaign's use of legitimate platforms for payload delivery.
- Execution parents in chronological order were 361GJX7J (PowerShell) and installer.exe.
- The file dropped AClient.exe.
- installer.exe dropped four malicious files: searchHost.exe, syshelpers.exe, nat1.vbs and runsys.vbs.
- The flagged IP 101[.]99[.]76[.]120 was flagged by 12 of 94 vendors and is hosted in Malaysia under AS45839 (Shinjiru Technology Sdn Bhd).
- A community comment tagged the IP as AsyncRAT C2, linking the related files to the AsyncRAT malware family.
- The original report is "From Trust to Threat: Hijacked Discord Invites Used for Multi-Stage Malware Delivery" by Check Point Research.
- Attackers hijacked expired and released Discord invite links through vanity link registration to redirect users to malicious servers.
- The campaign used the ClickFix phishing technique with multi-stage loaders and time-based evasion to deliver AsyncRAT and a customised Skuld Stealer targeting crypto wallets.
- ChromeKatz was used to bypass Chrome App Bound Encryption and steal cookies from newer Chromium browsers.

# Key Learning
- A single hash can open a full attack chain when you pivot on execution parents and dropped files.
- Community comments on threat intelligence platforms often provide the context that detection counts alone do not, such as campaign attribution and MITRE ATT&CK mapping.
- Traffic to trusted services like Bitbucket, GitHub, Pastebin and Discord should not be considered safe by default, as attackers use them to blend in.
- Low vendor detection counts on an IP do not mean it is benign. 12 of 94 was enough to confirm C2 activity once context was added.
- ClickFix relies on social engineering the user into running commands themselves, which bypasses many traditional delivery controls.

# What I Would Flag in a Real SOC
- Any outbound connection to 101[.]99[.]76[.]120 or other AsyncRAT C2 infrastructure, followed by host isolation and a full scope check.
- Any host with the collected hashes (syshelpers.exe, searchHost.exe, AClient.exe, nat1.vbs, runsys.vbs, installer.exe) present or executed.
- PowerShell spawning executables from user writable paths, especially following clipboard or run dialog activity linked to ClickFix lures.
- wscript or cscript running VBS files from temp or AppData folders.
- Downloads of executables from Bitbucket or S3 buckets by non developer endpoints.
- Unexpected access to browser cookie stores or Chromium process memory, which could indicate ChromeKatz or similar stealers.
- Users arriving at Discord invite links from external sources, used as a threat hunting pivot during the investigation.
